Thus $\expect{\bigl(X(\sigma)-X_{\mathrm{cm}}\bigr)^2}_0
  \sim
  \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\cos^2(n\sigma)}{n}.$ At a generic point along the string, or after averaging over $\sigma$, $\cos^2(n\sigma)$ contributes roughly $1/2$. With a mode cutoff $N$, $\expect{\bigl(X-X_{\mathrm{cm}}\bigr)^2}_{0,N}
  \sim
  \frac{1}{2}\sum_{n=1}^{N}\frac{1}{n}
  =\frac{1}{2}\log N+O(1).$

